			<div class="content" id="post-content-4830303">
									

<p>I have the same problem with this that I do with a lot of &quot;[religious, folk lore, or pop cultural thing] is back, <em>in SCP format</em>&quot; articles. Namely, that much of the enjoyment and understanding that is derived from the specific ways in which the story is told and uses its medium. Stripped of the specific story-telling context, and not adapted to the medium of a SCP document, it loses almost all of its value as anything but a reference. Just as I wouldn't be interested in reading <em>The Cabinet of Dr. Caligari</em>, The SCP Article, I wasn't particularly interested in reading this.</p>
<p>I will say that I enjoyed the dreamy quality of the recovered footage, but the article overall did little to nothing for me.</p>

